    "lawSection": "Election Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5447C\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto amend the election law, in relation to\nthe alteration of election districts by the board of\nelections; and to repeal certain provisions of such law\nrelating thereto\n\nPURPOSE:\nThe bill gives county boards of elections the flexibility to designate\nand change polling places based on the technology being provided by\nnew voting systems.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\nSection 1: Amends Election Law section 4-100(2) to give the county\nboards of elections throughout the State the same authority to\ncreate, consolidate, divide and alter election districts, which is\nalready granted to the boards of elections in the cities of Buffalo\nand New York and the counties of Monroe, Nassau and Suffolk.\n\nSection 2: Amends Election Law section 4-104(5) to add a new paragraph\n(d), which provides that a polling place using a new, certified\nvoting system may contain multiple election districts, provided that\nthe system prints out separate and distinct vote totals for each\nelection district in the polling station.\n\nSection 3: Amends Election Law section 4-104(6) to provide that a poll\nsite must be of sufficient size to accommodate voting systems and\nprivacy booths as required by the rules and regulations promulgated\nby the state board of elections, and that there must be a sufficient\nnumber of voting systems, election workers and other election\nresources to accommodate the number of voters eligible to vote in\nsuch polling place.\n\nSection 4: Repeals Election Law section 4-100(6).\n\nSection 5: Provides for the Act to take effect immediately.\n\nEXISTING LAW:\nNew bill.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nThe Federal Help America Vote Act and Election Reform and\nModernization Act of 2005 (Chapter 181/Laws of 2005) require that new\nvoting systems be implemented throughout New York State. Because\nthese systems are capable of handling ballots for multiple election\ndistricts, when designating polling places the county boards of\nelections need the flexibility to consider those capabilities and the\nability of the site to accommodate the number of voting systems,\nprivacy booths, election workers and other election resources in\nsufficient numbers to adequately serve the voters eligible to vote in\nthat polling place.\n\nThe repealed Election Law section 4-100(6), which provided for\ncounty boards of elections to establish election districts when\n\n\nlegislatures and other bodies fail to do so, has been made rendered\nobsolete by the powers granted to them by this bill.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nNew Bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nNone to the State.\n\nLOCAL FISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nNone. This bill will likely result in cost savings because it will\nallow boards of elections to consolidate polling places and reduce\nthe cost of running elections.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n\n",
